  "erratum": {
    "audit": "11162 files / 12471 records scanned across production capture, canary capture, eval captures, eval results, eval batches and profiles. 12 group id occurrences, 1 non-conforming — this test vector. No formal record was affected, so no record was downgraded and no id was rewritten.",
    "date": "2026-08-18",
    "id": "control-context-v1-erratum-1",
    "not_a_protocol_change": "No version bump: group_id() always produced 16 lowercase hex characters. The 15-character vector was defective test data, not protocol behaviour anyone should keep supporting.",
    "previous_sha256": "1a16ff299d425c3b09c678fc35857d141112c9851959270859f3d4f8130014b8",
    "resolution": "Enforce ^hmac-sha256:[0-9a-f]{16}$ in the BrainClaw verifier, the Gateway capability verifier and signer, and the DramaClaw opaque scope; regenerate every vector with the real signer; add negatives for raw ids, 15/17 characters, non-hex, uppercase, empty suffix and a wrong algorithm prefix.",
    "what": "The previous positive vector carried a project_group_id with 15 hex characters. group_id() is hexdigest()[:16] and can only emit 16, so that value was hand-written and violated the generation contract it was meant to demonstrate.",
    "why_it_mattered": "BrainClaw's verifier only checked the 'hmac-sha256:' prefix and a non-empty suffix — loose enough to accept the defective vector, and therefore loose enough to accept a raw identifier behind the prefix. An external caller could not exploit this without a valid Gateway->BrainClaw signature; the exposure is the signing path itself, any future trusted issuer, and defence in depth."
  },
